  "image_name": "1e8146c34a55fd7044b481b211a5dff0.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how the tumor microenvironment suppresses anti-tumor T-cell activity through both immune signaling and physical exclusion. At the center is a T cell surrounded by tumor cells and several other cell types, including dendritic cells, macrophages, regulatory T cells, and myeloid-derived suppressor cells. These neighboring cells appear to inhibit T cells through checkpoint interactions such as PD1/PD-L1 and other inhibitory receptors like LAG3 and TIGIT, while also releasing suppressive soluble factors including IL-10, TGFβ, VEGF, and IDO. This combination is linked to T-cell exhaustion.\n\nThe lower part of the figure broadens this idea to the whole tumor mass. It shows T cells facing hostile chemical and physical conditions inside the tumor, including low oxygen, low pH, high viscosity, and high pressure. The tumor is enclosed by fibroblasts and extracellular matrix, forming a dense barrier that seems to hinder T-cell penetration. A blood vessel at the bottom contains T cells that appear unable to leave the vasculature efficiently, further limiting immune access. Overall, the image conveys that tumors evade immunity not only by directly switching off T cells, but also by creating a difficult environment that blocks their entry and function.",
